WEBVTT

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:05.201
AI code assistants like Copilot suggest
completions, but they make mistakes,

00:00:05.281 --> 00:00:09.731
and programmers have to catch those mistakes before
they turn into bugs.

00:00:09.811 --> 00:00:14.764
One popular idea is to highlight the tokens the
model was least confident about,

00:00:14.844 --> 00:00:16.777
so a person knows where to look.

00:00:16.857 --> 00:00:23.508
This paper asks a sharp question: are those
generation probabilities actually the right thing to highlight?

00:00:23.588 --> 00:00:26.905
Through a preregistered study with thirty programmers,

00:00:26.985 --> 00:00:32.253
the authors compare highlighting low-probability
tokens against a new edit model that

00:00:32.333 --> 00:00:35.965
instead predicts which tokens a person is likely to
change.

00:00:36.045 --> 00:00:40.368
The edit model wins on speed, precision of edits,
and user preference,

00:00:40.448 --> 00:00:44.080
showing that generation probabilities alone are not
enough.

00:00:44.460 --> 00:00:48.670
AI code assistants are now everywhere, but they are
imperfect,

00:00:48.750 --> 00:00:52.960
and a wrong suggestion can quietly plant a bug or a
security hole.

00:00:53.040 --> 00:00:57.250
To catch those mistakes, a programmer first has to
notice them, and that is hard,

00:00:57.330 --> 00:01:01.540
because people tend to over-trust automation.

00:01:01.620 --> 00:01:05.830
The tricky part is that a single code suggestion is
not one decision but hundreds of tiny ones,

00:01:05.910 --> 00:01:10.120
one per token.

00:01:10.200 --> 00:01:14.410
Earlier research on communicating AI uncertainty
was built for single-shot decisions like a diagnosis,

00:01:14.490 --> 00:01:18.700
and it does not obviously carry over to this
token-by-token world.

00:01:19.080 --> 00:01:22.201
One natural idea is to highlight uncertain tokens,

00:01:22.281 --> 00:01:25.466
much like a spell-checker underlines suspect words,

00:01:25.546 --> 00:01:29.820
so the programmer's eye is drawn to the spots that
most need review.

00:01:29.900 --> 00:01:35.262
The obvious signal to use is the model's own
generation probability: tokens the model

00:01:35.342 --> 00:01:37.631
was least sure about get highlighted.

00:01:37.711 --> 00:01:42.176
This is an existing strategy, and it even ships in
OpenAI's Playground.

00:01:42.256 --> 00:01:49.091
But nobody had really tested whether low
probability actually lines up with where humans need to make edits,

00:01:49.171 --> 00:01:52.740
and that gap is exactly what this paper set out to probe.

00:01:53.120 --> 00:01:57.885
The authors ran a preregistered, mixed-methods
study with thirty programmers,

00:01:57.965 --> 00:02:03.295
comparing three ways of presenting the same AI code
completions: no highlights at all,

00:02:03.375 --> 00:02:08.643
highlights based on generation probability, and
highlights based on a new edit model.

00:02:08.723 --> 00:02:13.551
The edit model is the key idea: instead of asking
how confident the model was,

00:02:13.631 --> 00:02:17.452
it predicts which tokens a human is actually likely
to change.

00:02:17.532 --> 00:02:19.465
This reframes the whole problem,

00:02:19.545 --> 00:02:23.555
from surfacing model uncertainty to surfacing human
intervention,

00:02:23.635 --> 00:02:30.790
and the authors argue the same recipe could scale
up by learning from edit telemetry that products already collect.

00:02:31.170 --> 00:02:35.067
Under the hood, all three tools used the very same
Codex model,

00:02:35.147 --> 00:02:39.044
so any difference in behavior comes purely from how
the completion was displayed.

00:02:39.124 --> 00:02:43.021
The generation-probability tool highlighted tokens
the model was least confident in,

00:02:43.101 --> 00:02:46.998
at a seventy-one percent threshold.

00:02:47.078 --> 00:02:50.975
The edit-model tool highlighted tokens most likely
to be edited,

00:02:51.055 --> 00:02:54.952
at a sixty-six percent threshold,

00:02:55.032 --> 00:02:58.929
using a model trained on nine coders who had
previously edited Codex output until their tasks passed.

00:02:59.009 --> 00:03:02.906
Crucially,

00:03:02.986 --> 00:03:06.883
the thresholds were chosen so that every condition
showed the same total number of highlights,

00:03:06.963 --> 00:03:10.860
so the comparison is about which tokens get
highlighted, not how many.

00:03:11.240 --> 00:03:13.086
Thirty participants took part,

00:03:13.166 --> 00:03:17.452
all experienced Python programmers at a large US
technology company,

00:03:17.532 --> 00:03:21.176
each paid fifty dollars for roughly an hour of their time.

00:03:21.256 --> 00:03:25.928
Every person solved three coding problems drawn
from LeetCode's easy tier,

00:03:26.008 --> 00:03:27.918
with a ten-minute cap per task,

00:03:27.998 --> 00:03:33.119
and could run their code and a set of provided unit
tests to debug along the way.

00:03:33.199 --> 00:03:38.962
Task order and which tool went with which task were
randomized to keep the comparison fair.

00:03:39.042 --> 00:03:43.970
The edit model itself was trained earlier, on a
separate group of nine coders.

00:03:44.350 --> 00:03:46.404
The headline result is about speed.

00:03:46.484 --> 00:03:49.880
People finished fastest when using edit-model highlights,

00:03:49.960 --> 00:03:52.562
at about eight point six minutes on average,

00:03:52.642 --> 00:03:57.684
and slowest with generation-probability highlights,
at about nine point six minutes,

00:03:57.764 --> 00:04:00.794
with the no-highlight condition sitting in between.

00:04:00.874 --> 00:04:05.062
The gap between the two highlighting strategies is
highly significant,

00:04:05.142 --> 00:04:07.806
with a p-value of zero point zero zero three.

00:04:07.886 --> 00:04:14.208
What makes this striking is that
generation-probability highlighting was actually the worst of the three,

00:04:14.288 --> 00:04:17.013
slower even than showing no highlights at all,

00:04:17.093 --> 00:04:20.610
so highlighting the wrong tokens can hurt rather
than help.

00:04:20.990 --> 00:04:24.477
To understand why, the authors tracked which tokens
survived,

00:04:24.557 --> 00:04:28.045
meaning they were left unchanged by the participant.

00:04:28.125 --> 00:04:31.612
Under the edit model, tokens it left un-highlighted
survived far more often,

00:04:31.692 --> 00:04:35.179
about eighty-seven percent of the time,

00:04:35.259 --> 00:04:38.746
while tokens it did highlight survived only about
thirty-five percent of the time.

00:04:38.826 --> 00:04:42.314
Compare that to generation-probability highlighting,

00:04:42.394 --> 00:04:45.881
where highlighted tokens still survived
seventy-four percent of the time.

00:04:45.961 --> 00:04:49.448
In other words,

00:04:49.528 --> 00:04:53.015
the edit model's highlights closely predict what
people actually change,

00:04:53.095 --> 00:04:56.583
while generation-probability highlights barely move
the needle,

00:04:56.663 --> 00:05:00.150
and all of these differences are extremely significant.

00:05:00.530 --> 00:05:06.718
To put numbers on it: task time dropped from nine
point six one minutes with generation-probability

00:05:06.798 --> 00:05:10.897
highlighting to eight point five nine minutes with
the edit model,

00:05:10.977 --> 00:05:14.949
a significant difference at p equals zero point
zero zero three.

00:05:15.029 --> 00:05:20.078
Highlighted-token survival fell from seventy-four
percent to thirty-five percent,

00:05:20.158 --> 00:05:22.991
showing far tighter alignment with real edits,

00:05:23.071 --> 00:05:25.587
at p below zero point zero zero zero one.

00:05:25.667 --> 00:05:27.993
And on a seven-point preference scale,

00:05:28.073 --> 00:05:34.957
users rated the edit highlights three point nine
four versus two point eight eight for generation probability,

00:05:35.037 --> 00:05:38.060
significant at p equals zero point zero zero one.

00:05:38.440 --> 00:05:41.621
The lesson is simple but pointed.

00:05:41.701 --> 00:05:44.882
What you highlight matters more than whether you
highlight,

00:05:44.962 --> 00:05:48.143
and the right thing to highlight is where people
are likely to edit,

00:05:48.223 --> 00:05:51.404
not where the model happened to be unsure.

00:05:51.484 --> 00:05:54.666
Generation probabilities alone are not enough.

00:05:54.746 --> 00:05:57.927
Encouragingly,

00:05:58.007 --> 00:06:01.188
tools like Copilot already log the edits people
make to AI suggestions,

00:06:01.268 --> 00:06:04.449
so that same signal could train an open-world edit
model and carry these speed,

00:06:04.529 --> 00:06:07.710
precision, and preference gains into everyday coding.
